  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1239 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWe should see the coolest temperatures late Sunday night into Monday\nmorning with lows near freezing from the central South Plains to \nthe north and west. It is not out of the question that Lubbock \ncould receive its first (light) freeze Monday morning. High \ntemperatures should remain stuck in the 40s and 50s both Sunday \nand Monday. And it looks likely that Tuesday will also start out \ncloudy, and perhaps foggy as a WAA pattern sets up. It is \nuncertain what degree of clearing we might see by late Tuesday and\nhighs may remain once again mired in the 50s if we don't break \nout.\n\nBy Wednesday, a fast-moving shortwave will be approaching from the\nwest. Increasingly swift and deep S-SW flow should allow the area\nto finally break free of the clouds and experience a modest \nwarming trend back into the 60s. The shortwave will minor out as \nit moves across the southern Rockies and it doesn't appear that it\nwill support any rain chances for our area. However, a much \nlarger trough will be moving into the western states mid to late \nweek and this will maintain southwest flow aloft and south- \nsoutheasterly low-level flow at the surface over West Texas which\ncould bring some daily bouts of low stratus and/or fog as low-\nlevel moisture moves back in. As the upper low moves toward the \n4-corners region Friday we may see our rain chances increase, \nhowever uncertainty in the track of the system increases greatly \nheading into the weekend.",
